quantitykind:DataRate a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Data Rate"@en, "Data Rate"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:DataRate", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:DataRate";
  dct:description "The frequency derived from the period of time required to transmit one bit. This represents the amount of data transferred per second by a communications channel or a computing or storage device. Data rate is measured in units of bits per second (written \"b/s\" or \"bps\"), bytes per second (Bps), or baud. When applied to data rate, the multiplier prefixes \"kilo-\", \"mega-\", \"giga-\", etc. (and their abbreviations, \"k\", \"M\", \"G\", etc.) always denote powers of 1000. For example, 64 kbps is 64,000 bits per second. This contrasts with units of storage which use different prefixes to denote multiplication by powers of 1024, for example 1 kibibit = 1024 bits."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "The frequency derived from the period of time required to transmit one bit. This represents the amount of data transferred per second by a communications channel or a computing or storage device. Data rate is measured in units of bits per second (written \"b/s\" or \"bps\"), bytes per second (Bps), or baud. When applied to data rate, the multiplier prefixes \"kilo-\", \"mega-\", \"giga-\", etc. (and their abbreviations, \"k\", \"M\", \"G\", etc.) always denote powers of 1000. For example, 64 kbps is 64,000 bits per second. This contrasts with units of storage which use different prefixes to denote multiplication by powers of 1024, for example 1 kibibit = 1024 bits."^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:InformationFlowRate, quantitykind:InformationFlowRate;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Data_rate"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Data_rate"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T-1D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T-1D0;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "The frequency derived from the period of time required to transmit one bit. This represents the amount of data transferred per second by a communications channel or a computing or storage device. Data rate is measured in units of bits per second (written \"b/s\" or \"bps\"), bytes per second (Bps), or baud. When applied to data rate, the multiplier prefixes \"kilo-\", \"mega-\", \"giga-\", etc. (and their abbreviations, \"k\", \"M\", \"G\", etc.) always denote powers of 1000. For example, 64 kbps is 64,000 bits per second. This contrasts with units of storage which use different prefixes to denote multiplication by powers of 1024, for example 1 kibibit = 1024 bits.",
    "The frequency derived from the period of time required to transmit one bit. This represents the amount of data transferred per second by a communications channel or a computing or storage device. Data rate is measured in units of bits per second (written \"b/s\" or \"bps\"), bytes per second (Bps), or baud. When applied to data rate, the multiplier prefixes \"kilo-\", \"mega-\", \"giga-\", etc. (and their abbreviations, \"k\", \"M\", \"G\", etc.) always denote powers of 1000. For example, 64 kbps is 64,000 bits per second. This contrasts with units of storage which use different prefixes to denote multiplication by powers of 1024, for example 1 kibibit = 1024 bits.";
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:BIT-PER-SEC, unit:BIT-PER-SEC, unit:ExaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:ExaBIT-PER-SEC,
    unit:GigaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:GigaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:KiloBIT-PER-SEC, unit:KiloBIT-PER-SEC,
    unit:KiloBYTE-PER-SEC, unit:KiloBYTE-PER-SEC, unit:MegaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:MegaBIT-PER-SEC,
    unit:PetaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:PetaBIT-PER-SEC, unit:TeraBIT-PER-SEC, unit:TeraBIT-PER-SEC .

unit:BQ a qudt:Unit, qudt:Unit, qudt:DerivedUnit, qudt:DerivedUnit;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/unit>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit>;
  rdfs:label "Becquerel"@de, "Becquerel"@de, "becquerel"@cs, "becquerel"@cs, "becquerel"@en,
    "becquerel"@en, "becquerel"@es, "becquerel"@es, "becquerel"@fr, "becquerel"@fr, "becquerel"@hu,
    "becquerel"@hu, "becquerel"@it, "becquerel"@it, "becquerel"@ms, "becquerel"@ms, "becquerel"@pt,
    "becquerel"@pt, "becquerel"@ro, "becquerel"@ro, "becquerel"@sl, "becquerel"@sl, "becquerelium"@la,
    "becquerelium"@la, "bekerel"@pl, "bekerel"@pl, "bekerel"@tr, "bekerel"@tr, "μπεκερέλ"@el,
    "μπεκερέλ"@el, "бекерел"@bg, "бекерел"@bg, "беккерель"@ru, "беккерель"@ru, "בקרל"@he,
    "בקרל"@he, "بيكريل"@ar, "بيكريل"@ar, "بکرل"@fa, "بکرل"@fa, "बैक्वेरल"@hi, "बैक्वेरल"@hi,
    "ベクレル"@ja, "ベクレル"@ja, "贝克勒尔"@zh, "贝克勒尔"@zh;
  dct:description """
  The $\\textit{Becquerel}$ is the SI derived unit of activity, usually meaning radioactivity.
  Radioactivity is caused when atoms disintegrate, ejecting energetic particles. 
  One becquerel is the radiation caused by one disintegration per second; 
  this is equivalent to about $27.0270 \\text{picocuries (pCi)}$. 
  The unit is named for a French physicist, Antoine-Henri Becquerel (1852-1908), 
  the discoverer of radioactivity. 
  Note: both the becquerel and the hertz are basically defined as one event per second, 
  yet they measure different things. 
  The hertz is used to measure the rates of events that happen periodically in a fixed and definite cycle. 
  The becquerel is used to measure the rates of events that happen sporadically and unpredictably, 
  not in a definite cycle.
  """^^qudt:LatexString,
    """
  The $\\textit{Becquerel}$ is the SI derived unit of activity, usually meaning radioactivity.
  Radioactivity is caused when atoms disintegrate, ejecting energetic particles. 
  One becquerel is the radiation caused by one disintegration per second; 
  this is equivalent to about $27.0270 \\text{picocuries (pCi)}$. 
  The unit is named for a French physicist, Antoine-Henri Becquerel (1852-1908), 
  the discoverer of radioactivity. 
  Note: both the becquerel and the hertz are basically defined as one event per second, 
  yet they measure different things. 
  The hertz is used to measure the rates of events that happen periodically in a fixed and definite cycle. 
  The becquerel is used to measure the rates of events that happen sporadically and unpredictably, 
  not in a definite cycle.
  """^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-EMU>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-EMU>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-GAUSS>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS-GAUSS>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/IMPERIAL>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/IMPERIAL>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/USCS>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/USCS>;
  qudt:conversionMultiplier 1.0, 1.0;
  qudt:conversionMultiplierSN 1.0E0, 1.0E0;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Becquerel"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Becquerel"^^xsd:anyURI;
