unit:DIOPTER a qudt:Unit, qudt:Unit;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/unit>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit>;
  rdfs:label "Diopter"@en, "Diopter"@en;
  dct:description """
  The unit $\\textit{Dioptre}$, or $\\textit{Diopter}$, is a unit of measurement for the optical power of a lens or curved mirror,
   which is equal to the reciprocal of the focal length measured in metres (that is, $1/metre$). 
  For example, a $3 \\; dioptre$ lens brings parallel rays of light to focus at $1/3\\,metre$. 
  The same unit is also sometimes used for other reciprocals of distance, particularly radii of curvature
   and the vergence of optical beams. 
  Though the diopter is based on the SI-metric system it has not been included in the standard so that there is
   no international name or abbreviation for this unit of measurement within the international system of units
    this unit for optical power would need to be specified explicitly as the inverse metre.
  """^^qudt:LatexString,
    """
  The unit $\\textit{Dioptre}$, or $\\textit{Diopter}$, is a unit of measurement for the optical power of a lens or curved mirror,
   which is equal to the reciprocal of the focal length measured in metres (that is, $1/metre$). 
  For example, a $3 \\; dioptre$ lens brings parallel rays of light to focus at $1/3\\,metre$. 
  The same unit is also sometimes used for other reciprocals of distance, particularly radii of curvature
   and the vergence of optical beams. 
  Though the diopter is based on the SI-metric system it has not been included in the standard so that there is
   no international name or abbreviation for this unit of measurement within the international system of units
    this unit for optical power would need to be specified explicitly as the inverse metre.
  """^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/CGS>;
  qudt:conversionMultiplier 1.0, 1.0;
  qudt:conversionMultiplierSN 1.0E0, 1.0E0;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dioptre"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Dioptre"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L-1I0M0H0T0D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L-1I0M0H0T0D0;
  qudt:hasQuantityKind quantitykind:Curvature, quantitykind:Curvature;
  qudt:iec61360Code "0112/2///62720#UAB371", "0112/2///62720#UAB371";
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioptre?oldid=492506920"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dioptre?oldid=492506920"^^xsd:anyURI, "https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec62720/iec62720.nsf/Units/0112-2---62720%23UAB371"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec62720/iec62720.nsf/Units/0112-2---62720%23UAB371"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "D", "D";
  qudt:ucumCode "[diop]"^^qudt:UCUMcs, "[diop]"^^qudt:UCUMcs;
  qudt:uneceCommonCode "Q25", "Q25";
  qudt:scalingOf unit:PER-M .

quantitykind:Curvature a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Curvature"@en, "Curvature"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Curvature", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Curvature";
  dct:description "The canonical example of extrinsic curvature is that of a circle, which has curvature equal to the inverse of its radius everywhere. Smaller circles bend more sharply, and hence have higher curvature. The curvature of a smooth curve is defined as the curvature of its osculating circle at each point. The osculating circle of a sufficiently smooth plane curve at a given point on the curve is the circle whose center lies on the inner normal line and whose curvature is the same as that of the given curve at that point. This circle is tangent to the curve at the given point. The magnitude of curvature at points on physical curves can be measured in $diopters$ (also spelled $dioptre$) — this is the convention in optics."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "The canonical example of extrinsic curvature is that of a circle, which has curvature equal to the inverse of its radius everywhere. Smaller circles bend more sharply, and hence have higher curvature. The curvature of a smooth curve is defined as the curvature of its osculating circle at each point. The osculating circle of a sufficiently smooth plane curve at a given point on the curve is the circle whose center lies on the inner normal line and whose curvature is the same as that of the given curve at that point. This circle is tangent to the curve at the given point. The magnitude of curvature at points on physical curves can be measured in $diopters$ (also spelled $dioptre$) — this is the convention in optics."^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:InverseLength, quantitykind:InverseLength;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Curvature"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Curvature"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L-1I0M0H0T0D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L-1I0M0H0T0D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curvature"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Curvature"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription """The canonical example of extrinsic curvature is that of a circle, which has curvature equal to the inverse of its radius everywhere. Smaller circles bend more sharply, and hence have higher curvature. The curvature of a smooth curve is defined as the curvature of its osculating circle at each point. The osculating circle of a sufficiently smooth plane curve at a given point on the curve is the circle whose center lies on the inner normal line and whose curvature is the same as that of the given curve at that point. This circle is tangent to the curve at the given point.
That is, given a point P on a smooth curve C, the curvature of C at P is defined to be 1/R where R is the radius of the osculating circle of C at P. The magnitude of curvature at points on physical curves can be measured in diopters (also spelled dioptre) — this is the convention in optics. [Wikipedia],""",
    """The canonical example of extrinsic curvature is that of a circle, which has curvature equal to the inverse of its radius everywhere. Smaller circles bend more sharply, and hence have higher curvature. The curvature of a smooth curve is defined as the curvature of its osculating circle at each point. The osculating circle of a sufficiently smooth plane curve at a given point on the curve is the circle whose center lies on the inner normal line and whose curvature is the same as that of the given curve at that point. This circle is tangent to the curve at the given point.
That is, given a point P on a smooth curve C, the curvature of C at P is defined to be 1/R where R is the radius of the osculating circle of C at P. The magnitude of curvature at points on physical curves can be measured in diopters (also spelled dioptre) — this is the convention in optics. [Wikipedia],""";
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:DIOPTER, unit:DIOPTER .

