quantitykind:ElectromagneticPermeability a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:seeAlso quantitykind:MagneticFieldStrength_H, quantitykind:MagneticFieldStrength_H,
    quantitykind:MagneticFluxDensity, quantitykind:MagneticFluxDensity, <http://qudt.org/vocab/constant/ElectromagneticPermeabilityOfVacuum>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/constant/ElectromagneticPermeabilityOfVacuum>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/constant/MagneticConstant>,
    <http://qudt.org/vocab/constant/MagneticConstant>;
  rdfs:label "Permeability"@en, "Permeability"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ElectromagneticPermeability",
    "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ElectromagneticPermeability";
  dct:description """
  $\\textit{Permeability}$ is the degree of magnetization of a material that responds linearly to an applied magnetic field. 
  In general permeability is a tensor-valued quantity. 
  The definition given applies to an isotropic medium. 
  For an anisotropic medium permeability is a second order tensor. 
  In electromagnetism, permeability is the measure of the ability of a material to support the formation of a magnetic field within itself. 
  In other words, it is the degree of magnetization that a material obtains in response to an applied magnetic field. 
  Magnetic permeability is typically represented by the Greek letter $\\mu$. 
  The term was coined in September, 1885 by Oliver Heaviside. 
  The reciprocal of magnetic permeability is $\\textit{Magnetic Reluctivity}$.
  """^^qudt:LatexString,
    """
  $\\textit{Permeability}$ is the degree of magnetization of a material that responds linearly to an applied magnetic field. 
  In general permeability is a tensor-valued quantity. 
  The definition given applies to an isotropic medium. 
  For an anisotropic medium permeability is a second order tensor. 
  In electromagnetism, permeability is the measure of the ability of a material to support the formation of a magnetic field within itself. 
  In other words, it is the degree of magnetization that a material obtains in response to an applied magnetic field. 
  Magnetic permeability is typically represented by the Greek letter $\\mu$. 
  The term was coined in September, 1885 by Oliver Heaviside. 
  The reciprocal of magnetic permeability is $\\textit{Magnetic Reluctivity}$.
  """^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Permeability"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Permeability"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E-2L1I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A0E-2L1I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permeability_(electromagnetism)"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Permeability_(electromagnetism)"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:exactMatch quantitykind:Permeability, quantitykind:Permeability;
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\mu$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\mu$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$\\mu = \\frac{B}{H}$,  where $B$ is magnetic flux density,  and $H$ is magnetic field strength."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$\\mu = \\frac{B}{H}$,  where $B$ is magnetic flux density,  and $H$ is magnetic field strength."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:H-PER-M, unit:H-PER-M, unit:H_Stat-PER-CentiM, unit:H_Stat-PER-CentiM,
    unit:MicroH-PER-M, unit:MicroH-PER-M, unit:NanoH-PER-M, unit:NanoH-PER-M .

