quantitykind:ChemicalAffinity a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Chemical Affinity"@en, "Chemical Affinity"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ChemicalAffinity", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ChemicalAffinity";
  dct:description "In chemical physics and physical chemistry, \"Chemical Affinity\" is the electronic property by which dissimilar chemical species are capable of forming chemical compounds. Chemical affinity can also refer to the tendency of an atom or compound to combine by chemical reaction with atoms or compounds of unlike composition."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "In chemical physics and physical chemistry, \"Chemical Affinity\" is the electronic property by which dissimilar chemical species are capable of forming chemical compounds. Chemical affinity can also refer to the tendency of an atom or compound to combine by chemical reaction with atoms or compounds of unlike composition."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_affinity"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chemical_affinity"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "A", "A";
  qudt:isoNormativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "In chemical physics and physical chemistry, \"Chemical Affinity\" is the electronic property by which dissimilar chemical species are capable of forming chemical compounds. Chemical affinity can also refer to the tendency of an atom or compound to combine by chemical reaction with atoms or compounds of unlike composition.",
    "In chemical physics and physical chemistry, \"Chemical Affinity\" is the electronic property by which dissimilar chemical species are capable of forming chemical compounds. Chemical affinity can also refer to the tendency of an atom or compound to combine by chemical reaction with atoms or compounds of unlike composition.";
  qudt:latexDefinition "$A = -\\sum \\nu_b\\mu_B$, where $\\nu_b$ is the stoichiometric number of substance $B$ and $\\mu_B$ is the chemical potential of substance $B$."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$A = -\\sum \\nu_b\\mu_B$, where $\\nu_b$ is the stoichiometric number of substance $B$ and $\\mu_B$ is the chemical potential of substance $B$."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:J-PER-MOL, unit:J-PER-MOL .

quantitykind:ElectricPolarizability a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Kepengkutuban elektrik"@ms, "Kepengkutuban elektrik"@ms, "Kutuplanabilirlik"@tr,
    "Kutuplanabilirlik"@tr, "Polarisabilité"@fr, "Polarisabilité"@fr, "Polarizabilidad"@es,
    "Polarizabilidad"@es, "Polarizovatelnost"@cs, "Polarizovatelnost"@cs, "Polaryzowalność"@pl,
    "Polaryzowalność"@pl, "electric polarizability"@en, "electric polarizability"@en,
    "elektrische Polarisierbarkeit"@de, "elektrische Polarisierbarkeit"@de, "polarizabilidade"@pt,
    "polarizabilidade"@pt, "polarizzabilità elettrica"@it, "polarizzabilità elettrica"@it,
    "Поляризуемость"@ru, "Поляризуемость"@ru, "قابلية استقطاب"@ar, "قابلية استقطاب"@ar,
    "قطبیت پذیری الکتریکی"@fa, "قطبیت پذیری الکتریکی"@fa, "分極率"@ja, "分極率"@ja, "極化性"@zh,
    "極化性"@zh;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ElectricPolarizability",
    "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:ElectricPolarizability";
  dct:description "\"Electric Polarizability\" is the relative tendency of a charge distribution, like the electron cloud of an atom or molecule, to be distorted from its normal shape by an external electric field, which is applied typically by inserting the molecule in a charged parallel-plate capacitor, but may also be caused by the presence of a nearby ion or dipole."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "\"Electric Polarizability\" is the relative tendency of a charge distribution, like the electron cloud of an atom or molecule, to be distorted from its normal shape by an external electric field, which is applied typically by inserting the molecule in a charged parallel-plate capacitor, but may also be caused by the presence of a nearby ion or dipole."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polarizability"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Polarizability"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:isoNormativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "\"Electric Polarizability\" is the relative tendency of a charge distribution, like the electron cloud of an atom or molecule, to be distorted from its normal shape by an external electric field, which is applied typically by inserting the molecule in a charged parallel-plate capacitor, but may also be caused by the presence of a nearby ion or dipole.",
    "\"Electric Polarizability\" is the relative tendency of a charge distribution, like the electron cloud of an atom or molecule, to be distorted from its normal shape by an external electric field, which is applied typically by inserting the molecule in a charged parallel-plate capacitor, but may also be caused by the presence of a nearby ion or dipole.";
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\alpha$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\alpha$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$\\alpha_{i,j} = \\frac{\\partial p_i}{\\partial E_j}$, where $p_i$ is the cartesian component along the $i-axis$ of the electric dipole moment induced by the applied electric field strength acting on the molecule, and $E_j$ is the component along the $j-axis$ of this electric field strength."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$\\alpha_{i,j} = \\frac{\\partial p_i}{\\partial E_j}$, where $p_i$ is the cartesian component along the $i-axis$ of the electric dipole moment induced by the applied electric field strength acting on the molecule, and $E_j$ is the component along the $j-axis$ of this electric field strength."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:J-PER-MOL, unit:J-PER-MOL .

quantitykind:MolarEnergy a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Molar Energy"@en, "Molar Energy"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:MolarEnergy", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:MolarEnergy";
  dct:description "\"Molar Energy\" is the total energy contained by a thermodynamic system. The unit is $$J/mol$$, also expressed as $$joule/mole$$,  or $$joules per mole$$. This unit is commonly used in the SI unit system. The quantity has the dimension of $$M \\cdot L^2 \\cdot  T^{-2} \\cdot N^{-1}$$ where $$M$$ is mass, $$L$$ is length, $$T$$ is time, and $$N$$ is amount of substance."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "\"Molar Energy\" is the total energy contained by a thermodynamic system. The unit is $$J/mol$$, also expressed as $$joule/mole$$,  or $$joules per mole$$. This unit is commonly used in the SI unit system. The quantity has the dimension of $$M \\cdot L^2 \\cdot  T^{-2} \\cdot N^{-1}$$ where $$M$$ is mass, $$L$$ is length, $$T$$ is time, and $$N$$ is amount of substance."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://www.efunda.com/glossary/units/units--molar_energy--joule_per_mole.cfm"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.efunda.com/glossary/units/units--molar_energy--joule_per_mole.cfm"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joule_per_mole"^^xsd:anyURI, "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Joule_per_mole"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:siExactMatch <https://si-digital-framework.org/SI/quantities/MOEG>, <https://si-digital-framework.org/quantities/MOEG>;
  qudt:symbol "U_M", "U_M";
  qudt:isoNormativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31894"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$U_m = \\frac{U}{n}$, where $U$ is internal energy and $n$ is amount of substance."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$U_m = \\frac{U}{n}$, where $U$ is internal energy and $n$ is amount of substance."^^qudt:LatexString;
  <http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/vaem#todo> "dimensions are wrong", "dimensions are wrong";
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:J-PER-MOL, unit:J-PER-MOL, unit:KiloCAL-PER-MOL, unit:KiloCAL-PER-MOL,
    unit:KiloJ-PER-MOL, unit:KiloJ-PER-MOL .

unit:J-PER-MOL-K a qudt:Unit, qudt:Unit, qudt:DerivedUnit, qudt:DerivedUnit;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/unit>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/unit>;
  rdfs:label "Joule per Mole Kelvin"@en, "Joule per Mole Kelvin"@en;
  dct:description "Energy needed to heat one mole of substance by 1 Kelvin, under standard conditions (not standard temperature and pressure STP). The standard molar entropy is usually given the symbol S, and has units of joules per mole kelvin ( $ J\\cdot mol^{-1} K^{-1}$). Unlike standard enthalpies of formation, the value of S is an absolute. That is, an element in its standard state has a nonzero value of S at room temperature."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "Energy needed to heat one mole of substance by 1 Kelvin, under standard conditions (not standard temperature and pressure STP). The standard molar entropy is usually given the symbol S, and has units of joules per mole kelvin ( $ J\\cdot mol^{-1} K^{-1}$). Unlike standard enthalpies of formation, the value of S is an absolute. That is, an element in its standard state has a nonzero value of S at room temperature."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>;
  qudt:conversionMultiplier 1.0, 1.0;
  qudt:conversionMultiplierSN 1.0E0, 1.0E0;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H-1T-2D0, dimensionvector:A-1E0L2I0M1H-1T-2D0;
  qudt:hasQuantityKind quantitykind:MolarHeatCapacity, quantitykind:MolarHeatCapacity,
    quantitykind:MolarEntropy, quantitykind:MolarEntropy;
  qudt:iec61360Code "0112/2///62720#UAA184", "0112/2///62720#UAA184";
  qudt:informativeReference "https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec62720/iec62720.nsf/Units/0112-2---62720%23UAA184"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://cdd.iec.ch/cdd/iec62720/iec62720.nsf/Units/0112-2---62720%23UAA184"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "J/(mol·K)", "J/(mol·K)";
  qudt:ucumCode "J.mol-1.K-1"^^qudt:UCUMcs, "J.mol-1.K-1"^^qudt:UCUMcs;
  qudt:uneceCommonCode "B16", "B16";
  qudt:expression "$J/(mol-K)$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$J/(mol-K)$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:derivedCoherentUnitOfSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>;
  qudt:definedUnitOfSystem <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/sou/SI>;
