quantitykind:StressIntensityFactor a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Stress Intensity Factor"@en, "Stress Intensity Factor"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:StressIntensityFactor", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:StressIntensityFactor";
  dct:description "In fracture mechanics, the stress intensity factor (K) is used to predict the stress state (\"stress intensity\") near the tip of a crack or notch caused by a remote load or residual stresses. It is a theoretical construct usually applied to a homogeneous, linear elastic material and is useful for providing a failure criterion for brittle materials, and is a critical technique in the discipline of damage tolerance. The concept can also be applied to materials that exhibit small-scale yielding at a crack tip."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "In fracture mechanics, the stress intensity factor (K) is used to predict the stress state (\"stress intensity\") near the tip of a crack or notch caused by a remote load or residual stresses. It is a theoretical construct usually applied to a homogeneous, linear elastic material and is useful for providing a failure criterion for brittle materials, and is a critical technique in the discipline of damage tolerance. The concept can also be applied to materials that exhibit small-scale yielding at a crack tip."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L-0dot5I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L-0dot5I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress_intensity_factor"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stress_intensity_factor"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "K", "K";
  qudt:plainTextDescription "In fracture mechanics, the stress intensity factor (K) is used to predict the stress state (\"stress intensity\") near the tip of a crack or notch caused by a remote load or residual stresses. It is a theoretical construct usually applied to a homogeneous, linear elastic material and is useful for providing a failure criterion for brittle materials, and is a critical technique in the discipline of damage tolerance. The concept can also be applied to materials that exhibit small-scale yielding at a crack tip.",
    "In fracture mechanics, the stress intensity factor (K) is used to predict the stress state (\"stress intensity\") near the tip of a crack or notch caused by a remote load or residual stresses. It is a theoretical construct usually applied to a homogeneous, linear elastic material and is useful for providing a failure criterion for brittle materials, and is a critical technique in the discipline of damage tolerance. The concept can also be applied to materials that exhibit small-scale yielding at a crack tip.";
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\K$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\K$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:MegaPA-M0dot5, unit:MegaPA-M0dot5, unit:PA-M0dot5, unit:PA-M0dot5 .

