  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PermittivityRatio", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PermittivityRatio";
  dct:description "\"Permittivity Ratio\" is the ratio of permittivity to the permittivity of a vacuum."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "\"Permittivity Ratio\" is the ratio of permittivity to the permittivity of a vacuum."^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:DimensionlessRatio, quantitykind:DimensionlessRatio;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Permittivity"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Permittivity"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:expression "$rel-permittivity$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$rel-permittivity$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "\"Permittivity Ratio\" is the ratio of permittivity to the permittivity of a vacuum.",
    "\"Permittivity Ratio\" is the ratio of permittivity to the permittivity of a vacuum.";
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\epsilon_r$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\epsilon_r$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$\\epsilon_r = \\epsilon / \\epsilon_0$, where $\\epsilon$ is permittivity and $\\epsilon_0$ is the electric constant."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$\\epsilon_r = \\epsilon / \\epsilon_0$, where $\\epsilon$ is permittivity and $\\epsilon_0$ is the electric constant."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:qkdvDenominator dimensionvector:A0E2L-3I0M-1H0T4D0, dimensionvector:A0E2L-3I0M-1H0T4D0;
  qudt:qkdvNumerator dimensionvector:A0E2L-3I0M-1H0T4D0, dimensionvector:A0E2L-3I0M-1H0T4D0;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:UNITLESS, unit:UNITLESS .

quantitykind:PoissonRatio a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Poisson Ratio"@en, "Poisson Ratio"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PoissonRatio", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PoissonRatio";
  dct:description "The Poisson Ratio is the negative ratio of transverse to axial strain. In fact, when a sample object is stretched (or squeezed), to an extension (or contraction) in the direction of the applied load, it corresponds a contraction (or extension) in a direction perpendicular to the applied load. The ratio between these two quantities is the Poisson's ratio."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "The Poisson Ratio is the negative ratio of transverse to axial strain. In fact, when a sample object is stretched (or squeezed), to an extension (or contraction) in the direction of the applied load, it corresponds a contraction (or extension) in a direction perpendicular to the applied load. The ratio between these two quantities is the Poisson's ratio."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson%27s_ratio"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Poisson%27s_ratio"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:isoNormativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31889"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31889"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "The Poisson Ratio is the negative ratio of transverse to axial strain. In fact, when a sample object is stretched (or squeezed), to an extension (or contraction) in the direction of the applied load, it corresponds a contraction (or extension) in a direction perpendicular to the applied load. The ratio between these two quantities is the Poisson's ratio.",
    "The Poisson Ratio is the negative ratio of transverse to axial strain. In fact, when a sample object is stretched (or squeezed), to an extension (or contraction) in the direction of the applied load, it corresponds a contraction (or extension) in a direction perpendicular to the applied load. The ratio between these two quantities is the Poisson's ratio.";
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\mu$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\mu$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$\\mu = \\frac{\\Delta \\delta}{\\Delta l}$, where $\\Delta \\delta$ is the lateral contraction and $\\Delta l$ is the elongation."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$\\mu = \\frac{\\Delta \\delta}{\\Delta l}$, where $\\Delta \\delta$ is the lateral contraction and $\\Delta l$ is the elongation."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:UNITLESS, unit:UNITLESS .

quantitykind:PowerFactor a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:seeAlso quantitykind:ActivePower, quantitykind:ActivePower, quantitykind:ApparentPower,
    quantitykind:ApparentPower;
  rdfs:label "Leistungsfaktor"@de, "Leistungsfaktor"@de, "Współczynnik mocy"@pl, "Współczynnik mocy"@pl,
    "facteur de puissance"@fr, "facteur de puissance"@fr, "factor de potencia"@es, "factor de potencia"@es,
    "factor de putere"@ro, "factor de putere"@ro, "faktor kuasa"@ms, "faktor kuasa"@ms,
    "fator de potência"@pt, "fator de potência"@pt, "fattore di potenza"@it, "fattore di potenza"@it,
    "güç faktörü"@tr, "güç faktörü"@tr, "power factor"@en, "power factor"@en, "Účiník"@cs,
    "Účiník"@cs, "Коэффициент_мощности"@ru, "Коэффициент_мощности"@ru, "ضریب توان"@fa,
    "ضریب توان"@fa, "معامل القدرة"@ar, "معامل القدرة"@ar, "शक्ति गुणांक"@hi, "शक्ति गुणांक"@hi,
    "力率"@ja, "力率"@ja, "功率因数"@zh, "功率因数"@zh;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PowerFactor", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:PowerFactor";
  dct:description "\"Power Factor\", under periodic conditions, is the ratio of the absolute value of the active power $P$ to the apparent power $S$."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "\"Power Factor\", under periodic conditions, is the ratio of the absolute value of the active power $P$ to the apparent power $S$."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&ievref=131-11-46"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.electropedia.org/iev/iev.nsf/display?openform&ievref=131-11-46"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:expression "$power-factor$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$power-factor$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\lambda$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\lambda$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$\\lambda = \\left | P \\right | / \\left | S \\right |$, where $P$ is active power and $S$ is apparent power."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$\\lambda = \\left | P \\right | / \\left | S \\right |$, where $P$ is active power and $S$ is apparent power."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:UNITLESS, unit:UNITLESS .

quantitykind:QualityFactor a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:seeAlso quantitykind:Resistance, quantitykind:Resistance, quantitykind:Impedance,
    quantitykind:Impedance;
  rdfs:label "Quality Factor"@en, "Quality Factor"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:QualityFactor", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:QualityFactor";
  dct:description "\"Quality Factor\", of a resonant circuit, is a measure of the \"goodness\" or quality of a resonant circuit. A higher value for this figure of merit correspondes to a more narrow bandwith, which is desirable in many applications. More formally, $Q$ is the ratio of power stored to power dissipated in the circuit reactance and resistance, respectively"^^qudt:LatexString,
    "\"Quality Factor\", of a resonant circuit, is a measure of the \"goodness\" or quality of a resonant circuit. A higher value for this figure of merit correspondes to a more narrow bandwith, which is desirable in many applications. More formally, $Q$ is the ratio of power stored to power dissipated in the circuit reactance and resistance, respectively"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T0D1;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/home/store/catalogue_tc/catalogue_detail.htm?csnumber=31891"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.sourcetronic.com/electrical-measurement-glossary/quality-factor.html"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.sourcetronic.com/electrical-measurement-glossary/quality-factor.html"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.allaboutcircuits.com/vol_2/chpt_6/6.html"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://www.allaboutcircuits.com/vol_2/chpt_6/6.html"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "Q", "Q";
  qudt:latexDefinition "If $\\underline{Z} = R + jX$, then $Q = \\left | X \\right |/R$, where $\\underline{Z}$ is impedance, $R$ is resistance, and $X$ is reactance."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "If $\\underline{Z} = R + jX$, then $Q = \\left | X \\right |/R$, where $\\underline{Z}$ is impedance, $R$ is resistance, and $X$ is reactance."^^qudt:LatexString;
  <http://www.linkedmodel.org/schema/vaem#todo> "Resolve Quality Facor - electronics and also doses",
    "Resolve Quality Facor - electronics and also doses";
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:UNITLESS, unit:UNITLESS .

