quantitykind:Work a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Arbeit"@de, "Arbeit"@de, "delo"@sl, "delo"@sl, "iş"@tr, "iş"@tr, "kerja"@ms,
    "kerja"@ms, "lavoro"@it, "lavoro"@it, "lucru mecanic"@ro, "lucru mecanic"@ro, "praca"@pl,
    "praca"@pl, "práce"@cs, "práce"@cs, "trabajo"@es, "trabajo"@es, "trabalho"@pt, "trabalho"@pt,
    "travail"@fr, "travail"@fr, "work"@en, "work"@en, "کار"@fa, "کار"@fa, "कार्य"@hi,
    "कार्य"@hi, "仕事量"@ja, "仕事量"@ja, "功"@zh, "功"@zh;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Energy", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Energy";
  dct:description """
  $\\textit{Work}$ or $net\\ work$ is equal to the change in kinetic energy. 
  This relationship is called the work-energy theorem: 
  $$Wnet = K. E._f  K. E._o $$
   where $K. E._f$ is the final kinetic energy and $K. E._o$ is the original kinetic energy. 
  Potential energy, also referred to as stored energy, is the ability of a system to do work due to its position or internal structure. 
  Change in potential energy is equal to work. 
  The potential energy equations can also be derived from the integral form of work:
  $$\\Delta P. E. = W = \\int  F \\cdot dx$$.
  """^^qudt:LatexString,
    """
  $\\textit{Work}$ or $net\\ work$ is equal to the change in kinetic energy. 
  This relationship is called the work-energy theorem: 
  $$Wnet = K. E._f  K. E._o $$
   where $K. E._f$ is the final kinetic energy and $K. E._o$ is the original kinetic energy. 
  Potential energy, also referred to as stored energy, is the ability of a system to do work due to its position or internal structure. 
  Change in potential energy is equal to work. 
  The potential energy equations can also be derived from the integral form of work:
  $$\\Delta P. E. = W = \\int  F \\cdot dx$$.
  """^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:Energy, quantitykind:Energy;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L2I0M1H0T-2D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Work_(physics)"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Work_(physics)"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://www.cliffsnotes.com/study_guide/Work-and-Energy.topicArticleId-10453,articleId-10418.html"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.cliffsnotes.com/study_guide/Work-and-Energy.topicArticleId-10453,articleId-10418.html"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "A", "A";
  qudt:isoNormativeReference "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31889"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://www.iso.org/iso/catalogue_detail?csnumber=31889"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "A force is said to do Work when it acts on a body so that there is a displacement of the point of application, however small, in the direction of the force.  The concepts of work and energy are closely tied to the concept of force because an applied force can do work on an object and cause a change in energy. Energy is defined as the ability to do work. Work is done on an object when an applied force moves it through a distance. Kinetic energy is the energy of an object in motion. The net work is equal to the change in kinetic energy.",
    "A force is said to do Work when it acts on a body so that there is a displacement of the point of application, however small, in the direction of the force.  The concepts of work and energy are closely tied to the concept of force because an applied force can do work on an object and cause a change in energy. Energy is defined as the ability to do work. Work is done on an object when an applied force moves it through a distance. Kinetic energy is the energy of an object in motion. The net work is equal to the change in kinetic energy.";
  qudt:latexDefinition "$A = \\int Pdt$, where $P$ is power and $t$ is time."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$A = \\int Pdt$, where $P$ is power and $t$ is time."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:AttoJ, unit:AttoJ, unit:BTU_IT, unit:BTU_IT, unit:BTU_TH,
    unit:BTU_TH, unit:CAL_IT, unit:CAL_IT, unit:CAL_TH, unit:CAL_TH, unit:ERG, unit:ERG,
    unit:EV, unit:EV, unit:E_h, unit:E_h, unit:ExaJ, unit:ExaJ, unit:FT-LB_F, unit:FT-LB_F,
