quantitykind:IncidenceProportion a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:seeAlso quantitykind:IncidenceRate, quantitykind:IncidenceRate;
  rdfs:label "Incidence Proportion"@en, "Incidence Proportion"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Frequency", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:Incidence";
  dct:description "Incidence proportion (also known as cumulative incidence) is the number of new cases within a specified time period divided by the size of the population initially at risk. For example, if a population initially contains 1,000 non-diseased persons and 28 develop a condition over two years of observation, the incidence proportion is 28 cases per 1,000 persons per two years, i.e. 2.8% per two years."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "Incidence proportion (also known as cumulative incidence) is the number of new cases within a specified time period divided by the size of the population initially at risk. For example, if a population initially contains 1,000 non-diseased persons and 28 develop a condition over two years of observation, the incidence proportion is 28 cases per 1,000 persons per two years, i.e. 2.8% per two years."^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:Incidence, quantitykind:Incidence;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T-1D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H0T-1D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incidence_(epidemiology)"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Incidence_(epidemiology)"^^xsd:anyURI, "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulative_incidence"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cumulative_incidence"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:plainTextDescription "Incidence proportion (also known as cumulative incidence) is the number of new cases within a specified time period divided by the size of the population initially at risk. For example, if a population initially contains 1,000 non-diseased persons and 28 develop a condition over two years of observation, the incidence proportion is 28 cases per 1,000 persons per two years, i.e. 2.8% per two years.",
    "Incidence proportion (also known as cumulative incidence) is the number of new cases within a specified time period divided by the size of the population initially at risk. For example, if a population initially contains 1,000 non-diseased persons and 28 develop a condition over two years of observation, the incidence proportion is 28 cases per 1,000 persons per two years, i.e. 2.8% per two years.";
