    "median relative change in length over a specific length of the test piece, divided by the temperature range caused by it"@en;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H-1T0D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L0I0M0H-1T0D0;
  qudt:symbol "0173-1#Z4-BAJ473#002", "0173-1#Z4-BAJ473#002";
  qudt:plainTextDescription "auf eine festgelegte Länge des Probekörpers bezogene mittlere relative Längenänderung dividiert durch die sie verursachende Temperaturänderung"@de,
    "auf eine festgelegte Länge des Probekörpers bezogene mittlere relative Längenänderung dividiert durch die sie verursachende Temperaturänderung"@de .

quantitykind:ThermalDiffusivity a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Thermal Diffusivity"@en, "Thermal Diffusivity"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:AreaPerTime", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:AreaPerTime";
  dct:description "In heat transfer analysis, thermal diffusivity (usually denoted $\\alpha$ but $a$, $\\kappa$,$k$, and $D$ are also used) is the thermal conductivity divided by density and specific heat capacity at constant pressure. The formula is: $\\alpha = {k \\over {\\rho c_p}}$, where k is thermal conductivity ($W/(\\mu \\cdot K)$), $\\rho$ is density ($kg/m^{3}$), and $c_p$ is specific heat capacity ($\\frac{J}{(kg \\cdot K)}$) .The denominator $\\rho c_p$, can be considered the volumetric heat capacity ($\\frac{J}{(m^{3} \\cdot K)}$)."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "In heat transfer analysis, thermal diffusivity (usually denoted $\\alpha$ but $a$, $\\kappa$,$k$, and $D$ are also used) is the thermal conductivity divided by density and specific heat capacity at constant pressure. The formula is: $\\alpha = {k \\over {\\rho c_p}}$, where k is thermal conductivity ($W/(\\mu \\cdot K)$), $\\rho$ is density ($kg/m^{3}$), and $c_p$ is specific heat capacity ($\\frac{J}{(kg \\cdot K)}$) .The denominator $\\rho c_p$, can be considered the volumetric heat capacity ($\\frac{J}{(m^{3} \\cdot K)}$)."^^qudt:LatexString;
  skos:broader quantitykind:AreaPerTime, quantitykind:AreaPerTime;
  qudt:dbpediaMatch "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Thermal_diffusivity"^^xsd:anyURI, "http://dbpedia.org/resource/Thermal_diffusivity"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:hasDimensionVector dimensionvector:A0E0L2I0M0H0T-1D0, dimensionvector:A0E0L2I0M0H0T-1D0;
  qudt:informativeReference "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal_diffusivity"^^xsd:anyURI,
    "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thermal_diffusivity"^^xsd:anyURI;
  qudt:symbol "a", "a";
  qudt:latexSymbol "$\\alpha$"^^qudt:LatexString, "$\\alpha$"^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:latexDefinition "$a = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\rho   c_\\rho}$, where $\\lambda$ is thermal conductivity, $\\rho$ is mass density and $c_\\rho$ is specific heat capacity at constant pressure."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "$a = \\frac{\\lambda}{\\rho   c_\\rho}$, where $\\lambda$ is thermal conductivity, $\\rho$ is mass density and $c_\\rho$ is specific heat capacity at constant pressure."^^qudt:LatexString;
  qudt:applicableUnit unit:CentiM2-PER-SEC, unit:CentiM2-PER-SEC, unit:FT2-PER-HR, unit:FT2-PER-HR,
    unit:FT2-PER-SEC, unit:FT2-PER-SEC, unit:IN2-PER-SEC, unit:IN2-PER-SEC, unit:M2-HZ,
    unit:M2-HZ, unit:M2-PER-SEC, unit:M2-PER-SEC, unit:MilliM2-PER-SEC, unit:MilliM2-PER-SEC .

quantitykind:ThermalEfficiency a qudt:QuantityKind, qudt:QuantityKind;
  rdfs:isDefinedBy <http://qudt.org/3.0.0/vocab/quantitykind>, <http://qudt.org/vocab/quantitykind>;
  rdfs:label "Thermal Efficiency"@en, "Thermal Efficiency"@en;
  rdfs:comment "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:DimensionlessRatio", "Applicable units are those of quantitykind:DimensionlessRatio";
  dct:description "Thermal efficiency is a dimensionless performance measure of a thermal device such as an internal combustion engine, a boiler, or a furnace. The input to the device is heat, or the heat-content of a fuel that is consumed. The desired output is mechanical work, or heat, or possibly both."^^qudt:LatexString,
    "Thermal efficiency is a dimensionless performance measure of a thermal device such as an internal combustion engine, a boiler, or a furnace. The input to the device is heat, or the heat-content of a fuel that is consumed. The desired output is mechanical work, or heat, or possibly both."^^qudt:LatexString;
